1. Optimized Linux File System Layout for 50 GB Server
The server has a total storage capacity of 50 GB and needs to store system logs, user files and application data. Since log files are growing rapidly, a separate partition should be provided for logs.
Proposed File System Layout
/boot – 1 GB – Boot files
/ – 8 GB – Operating system files
/var/log – 10 GB – System and application logs
/home – 10 GB – User files
/opt – 7 GB – Application data
Swap – 4 GB – Virtual memory
Free/Reserved Space – 10 GB – Emergency use
Total = 50 GB
The /var/log partition is limited to 10 GB, so log files cannot consume space belonging to the operating system or user files.
Log rotation can be implemented using the Linux logrotate utility. Logs can be rotated daily, compressed and old logs can be deleted after a fixed retention period.
Disk usage should be monitored regularly using tools such as df and du. Alerts can be generated when disk usage reaches 80% or 90%.
Other measures include:
Keep /var/log as a separate partition.
Compress old log files.
Delete or archive unnecessary logs.
Maintain reserved free space.
Monitor disk and inode usage.
Configure systemd journal limits if required.

2.Kernel-Level Optimization for High CPU Workloads
Frequent context switching reduces CPU performance because the processor spends more time switching between processes instead of executing useful work. Since hardware upgrades are not possible, kernel-level optimization should be used.
The following techniques can improve performance:
CPU Affinity: Assign CPU-intensive processes to specific CPU cores to reduce unnecessary CPU migration.
Scheduler Tuning: Adjust process priorities so that important applications receive CPU time first.
Real-Time Scheduling: Use scheduling policies such as SCHED_FIFO and SCHED_RR for real-time applications.
CPU Isolation: Reserve selected CPU cores for real-time applications so that other processes do not interfere.
Process Priority: Increase the priority of critical processes and reduce the priority of background processes.
Kernel Modules: Identify and remove unnecessary kernel modules to reduce resource usage. Critical modules should not be removed.
Disable Unnecessary Services: Stop services that are not required because they consume CPU and memory resources.
Kernel Parameter Tuning: Appropriate kernel parameters can be adjusted to improve scheduling and system responsiveness.

3.Processing 1 Million Log Entries Using Shell Scripting
Processing one million log entries per day requires an efficient method because processing every line individually can take a long time.
The best approach is to use AWK because it can process the entire log file in a single pass.
For example, AWK can be used to count ERROR, WARNING and INFO messages from the log file.
A suitable shell script can perform the following operations:
Check whether the log file exists.
Read the file using AWK.
Count different types of log messages.
Display the final results.
Measure execution time.
AWK is preferred because:
It processes the file sequentially in a single pass.
It avoids unnecessary process creation.
It can filter, count and extract fields efficiently.
It is suitable for processing millions of records.
It is faster than using shell loops for every log entry.
Unnecessary commands and repeated processing should be avoided. For example, using a loop to execute separate commands for every log entry creates significant overhead.
The execution time should be measured and the script should be optimized so that it completes within the required 2 minutes.

4. Managing a Faulty Process Consuming 90% CPU
A faulty process consuming 90% CPU can affect the performance of other services. Since the system cannot be restarted, the process must be controlled without affecting critical services.
The process management strategy is:
Identify the Process: Use tools such as top or ps to identify the process consuming excessive CPU.
Monitor the Process: Continuously monitor the process to determine whether the CPU usage remains high.
Check Dependencies: Verify whether the process is related to any critical service.
Reduce Priority: Lower the process priority so that critical processes receive CPU time.
CPU Affinity: Restrict the faulty process to selected CPU cores.
Graceful Termination: If the process continues causing problems, terminate it gracefully.
Force Termination: If the process does not respond, force termination can be used as a last option.
Verify Critical Services: After controlling the process, check that all critical services are still running properly.
The correct sequence is:
Identify → Monitor → Reduce Priority → Restrict CPU → Terminate if Necessary → Verify Services

5. RAM Allocation for 5 Virtual Machines
Given:
Total physical RAM = 16 GB
Number of virtual machines = 5
Minimum RAM required by each VM = 2 GB
RAM required by host OS = 4 GB
Calculation
RAM required by VMs:
5 × 2 GB = 10 GB
RAM required by host:
4 GB
Total required:
10 GB + 4 GB = 14 GB
Remaining RAM:
16 GB − 14 GB = 2 GB
Proposed Allocation
Host OS = 4 GB
VM1 = 2 GB
VM2 = 2 GB
VM3 = 2 GB
VM4 = 2 GB
VM5 = 2 GB
Reserved/Headroom = 2 GB
Total = 16 GB
Memory ballooning and dynamic memory allocation can be used to provide additional memory to VMs when required.
The host should always retain at least 4 GB because it is necessary for the operating system and virtualization overhead. The remaining 2 GB should be kept as reserve instead of allocating all memory to the VMs.

  
